; expect 55
; `#| ... |#` is trivia: it may sit between any two tokens, it nests,
; and its contents are not source. Two of the comments below hold text
; that would not parse if it were read as code, and one holds a `|#`
; that closes an inner comment rather than this one - so a scanner that
; stopped at the first `|#` would leave `still comment |#` in the token
; stream and this file would not parse at all.
;
; `fmt` moves a comment that sits INSIDE an expression to the end of
; the file. That is not new here and is not about block comments - a
; line comment in the same position moves the same way - so the
; formatted copy of this file that `check-fmt.sh` builds still runs and
; still answers 55.
(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  #| a comment before the body |#
  (+ #| between the head and its first argument |#
     50
     #| nested: #| (fn (this) would not parse |# still comment |#
     5))
